---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Legal Foundation" activePage="legal-foundation">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-tag">§ 02 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Article's Legal Foundation of Bail</div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div>
          <h1>The Legal<br/><span class="ital">Foundation</span> of Bail</h1>
          <p class="hero-sub">Jurisprudence of Bail: Navigating the Bailable, Non-Bailable, and Unbailable Under Malaysian Criminal Justice.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="crest" aria-label="Article record">
          <dl>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Volume</dt><dd>I</dd></div>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Section</dt><dd>§ 02</dd></div>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Type</dt><dd>Article</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <div class="crest-divider"></div>
          <div class="crest-num">03<small>Offence Categories</small></div>
          <div class="crest-divider"></div>
          <dl>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Subject</dt><dd>SOSMA</dd></div>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Reform</dt><dd>Yes</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Abstract -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.1 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Abstract</div>
          <div class="kicker">The argument</div>
        </div>
        <div class="measure">
          <p>Bail plays an important role in balancing an accused person's right to liberty with the need to protect public safety and ensure attendance in court.</p>
          <p>The law classifies offences into <em>bailable</em>, <em>non&#8209;bailable</em>, and <em>unbailable</em> categories, each carrying different levels of judicial discretion and legal consequences. Through statutory provisions and judicial decisions, Malaysian courts continuously navigate the tension between the presumption of innocence and the interests of justice.</p>
          <p>This study examines how bail jurisprudence operates within Malaysia's legal framework, particularly in cases involving serious offences and national security concerns.</p>
          <a class="article-link" href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qX2973OKI8LFi8dy3BG0swNcyVZQcQZDr5yeVb3mB3Y/edit?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="margin-top:24px;">
            <span>
              <span class="label">The Full Article</span>
              <strong>Read on Google Docs</strong>
            </span>
            <svg class="arrow" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M7 17 17 7"/><path d="M8 7h9v9"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Three Categories -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.2 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Framework</div>
          <h2 style="margin:0;">The Three<br/>Categories</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p style="font-size:20px; color:var(--ink-soft); margin:0;">The statutory framework arranges offences along a spectrum of judicial discretion — from an automatic entitlement to bail, through a careful weighing of factors, to a statutory denial reserved for the gravest crimes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="offence-grid">
        <article class="offence-cell">
          <span class="badge">Category I</span>
          <h3>Bailable Offence</h3>
          <p>A less severe offence where the accused is entitled to bail as a matter of right. The court cannot deny bail if the accused is ready to furnish it.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="offence-cell">
          <span class="badge">Category II</span>
          <h3>Non&#8209;Bailable Offence</h3>
          <p>A more serious offence where granting bail is at the discretion of the court. The judge weighs flight risk, the severity of the charge, and the likelihood of tampering with witnesses.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="offence-cell">
          <span class="badge">Category III</span>
          <h3>Unbailable Offence</h3>
          <p>Offences carrying the death penalty or life imprisonment, which generally cannot be granted bail under the statute.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SOSMA -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.3 &nbsp;·&nbsp; SOSMA</div>
          <h2 style="margin:0;">Security Offences<br/>(Special Measures) Act 2012</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="measure">
          <p>SOSMA is a strict Malaysian security law designed for terrorism and organised crime that completely bans judges from granting pre&#8209;trial bail, forcing accused individuals to remain in prison until their trial ends — a practice critics condemn as unfair "pre&#8209;trial punishment".</p>
          <p>The law allows a narrow exception for women, youths under eighteen, and the dangerously ill, though even these individuals face extremely harsh release conditions such as massive financial bonds and electronic ankle tracking.</p>
          <blockquote class="pull-large">"The judiciary recently drew a firm line against government overreach — ruling that an acquitted person must be freed immediately."</blockquote>
          <p>This absolute ban has caused a major constitutional tug&#8209;of&#8209;war over the separation of powers between Parliament and the courts. The judiciary recently struck down a rule that allowed the State to keep a person locked up even after a judge had found them innocent.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Opinion / Reform -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.4 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Opinion</div>
          <h2 style="margin:0;">A Pathway<br/>for Reform</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="measure">
          <p>The article draws on international jurisprudence to propose a clear pathway for reforming Malaysia's strict bail laws, focusing on how courts can prevent prolonged pre&#8209;trial detention from becoming an unconstitutional punishment.</p>
          <p>It argues that Malaysia should adopt the <em>Najeeb principle</em> for its SOSMA regime. Although SOSMA is heavily protected by Article 149 of the Federal Constitution, keeping an unconvicted person locked up indefinitely directly violates the fundamental right to life and liberty under Article 5(1).</p>
          <p>When security trials become excessively delayed, statutory prohibitions must yield to the constitutional right to a speedy trial — a concept already supported by early Malaysian jurisprudence — to ensure the State cannot transform pre&#8209;trial detention into unauthorised punishment.</p>
          <div class="case-row">
            <article class="case-cell">
              <p class="case-name">Charles v. Attorney General</p>
              <p class="case-loc">Trinidad &amp; Tobago</p>
              <p>Highlighted how total bail bans damage the separation of powers between the legislature and the judiciary.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-cell">
              <p class="case-name">Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb</p>
              <p class="case-loc">Supreme Court of India</p>
              <p>Constitutional courts retain inherent power to bypass strict statutory bail bans where trial is unreasonably delayed.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <p style="margin-top:30px; font-style:italic; color:var(--muted); font-size:16px;">See also: <strong>PP v. Dato' Mat Safuan</strong> — an early Malaysian authority supporting the constitutional right to a speedy trial.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>

<style>
.article {
  padding: 80px 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
}
.article:last-of-type { border-bottom: 0; }
.article-grid {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr; gap: 80px;
}
.article-grid p { margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink-soft); }
.article-grid p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
.article-grid h3 { font-size: 30px; font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 18px; letter-spacing: -.005em; }
.article-link {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px;
  border: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 18px 22px;
  text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink);
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13px;
  background: var(--surface);
  transition: all .15s ease;
  max-width: 520px;
}
.article-link:hover { background: var(--ink); color: var(--bg); }
.article-link .label {
  font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .22em; text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--muted); display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;
}
.article-link:hover .label { color: rgba(246,242,232,.6); }
.article-link strong { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 18px; font-style: italic; font-weight: 500; }
.article-link .arrow { margin-left: auto; }

.offence-grid {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 40px;
}
.offence-cell {
  background: var(--bg); padding: 40px 30px 36px; display: flex; flex-direction: column;
}
.offence-cell .badge {
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .22em;
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--gold-deep);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 5px 10px;
  align-self: flex-start; margin-bottom: 18px;
}
.offence-cell h3 { font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 14px; font-weight: 500; }
.offence-cell p { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }

.case-row {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 30px;
}
.case-cell { background: var(--bg); padding: 30px 28px; }
.case-cell .case-name { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 20px; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 8px; }
.case-cell .case-loc {
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .2em;
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 14px;
}
.case-cell p { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .article-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 36px; }
  .offence-grid, .case-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .article { padding: 60px 0; }
}
</style>
